.TH DF 1
.SH NAME
df \- report free disk space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
[\ \-tfvih\ ] [\ filesystem\ |\ file\ ...\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B df
reports the free space on each mounted file system, as the file systems
themselves record it in their super blocks. With no arguments it reports
on every file system in /etc/mnttab. A
.I filesystem
may be named by its device, as /dev/root, or by its name in /dev, as root;
a
.I file
or directory stands for the file system it is on. A file holding a
file system, such as the image of a floppy disk, may be named too.
.PP
Space is counted in blocks of 512 bytes, whatever the size of the file
system's own blocks. With no options, each file system takes a line:
.PP
.nf
	/       (/dev/root ):    22704 blocks    3686 i-nodes
.fi
.PP
the directory it is mounted on, its device, its free blocks and its
free i-nodes (the number of files that can still be made). The options
are:
.TP 6
.B \-t
Report the total blocks too, and how many hold the boot block, the super
block and the i-list:
.RS
.PP
.nf
	(   37580 total blocks,  592 for i-nodes)
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \-f
Report only the free blocks.
.TP
.B \-v
A table of the space: the size, the used, the free, and the percentage
used, in K, M, G and T as with
.BR \-h ,
where XENIX's own df counts blocks:
.RS
.PP
.nf
	Mount Dir   Filesystem    size    used    free  % used
	/           /dev/root      18M    9.9M    8.5M     54%
.fi
.RE
.IP
This is the table XENIX's installer, custom, shows for Display current
disk usage.
.TP
.B \-i
A table of the i-nodes: the used, the free, and the percentage used.
With
.BR \-v ,
both tables in one.
.TP
.B \-h
A table in sizes a person reads easily, in units of 1024: K for
kilobytes, M megabytes, G gigabytes and T terabytes, with a decimal
place below 10:
.RS
.PP
.nf
	Filesystem     Size   Used  Avail Capacity  Mounted on
	/dev/root       18M   7.2M    11M      39%  /
.fi
.RE
.PP
The forms other than \-v and \-h are those of XENIX's own df, to the
character, because other programs read them: custom reads the free
blocks from the first one.
.SH FILES
.TP 16
/etc/mnttab
the mounted file systems
.TP
/dev/*
the devices
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"cannot open" for a device df may not read; "not a XENIX file system";
"not found" for a name that is neither a file nor a device. The exit
status is 1 after any of these, 2 after a mistake in the options, and 0
otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
du(1), mount(1), fsck(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B df
command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. The
.B \-h
option, and the sizes in
.BR \-v ,
are the xenix86-devsys system tools'.
.SH AUTHORS
This df was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
df believes the count of free blocks the super block holds; with
.BR \-f ,
XENIX's df counts the free list instead, which can differ on a file
system that needs checking.
.PP
df must be able to read the devices, so it runs as the owner of them,
sysinfo.
